INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L1 — Projected unit credit actuarial cost method
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715, which actuarial cost method is required to measure the projected benefit obligation of a defined benefit pension plan?', '["Entry age normal method", "Aggregate cost method", "Projected unit credit method", "Frozen initial liability method"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 715-30-35-6 requires the projected unit credit method (also called the benefit/years-of-service method) to attribute pension benefits to periods of employee service. Unlike the aggregate cost method (Choice B), which spreads costs evenly across all periods, the projected unit credit method assigns a separate unit of benefit to each service year, providing the most precise measure of the earned obligation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — First-year pension expense for newly adopted plan
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Hartwell Corp. adopts a new defined benefit pension plan on January 1 with no retroactive benefits. The service cost for the year is $280,000, the discount rate is 5%, and plan assets are zero at inception. By year-end, Hartwell contributes $250,000 to the plan trust. What is Hartwell''s net periodic pension cost for the first year?', '["$250,000", "$280,000", "$280,000 minus the return on the $250,000 mid-year contribution", "$30,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-4, net periodic pension cost includes service cost, interest cost, expected return, and amortization items. In the first year of a new plan with no retroactive benefits, both beginning PBO and plan assets are zero — so interest cost is zero (5% x $0), expected return is zero, and no prior service cost or actuarial loss exists. Pension expense equals service cost of $280,000. The $250,000 contribution affects funded status, not expense. Choice A incorrectly equates contributions with expense.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Business combination acquiring company with underfunded DB plan
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Pinnacle Industries acquires Summit Corp. in a business combination. Summit sponsors a defined benefit plan with a PBO of $9,200,000 and plan assets of $7,400,000. Summit''s balance sheet reports a net pension liability of $1,800,000 and accumulated OCI losses of $620,000 related to the plan. Under ASC 805 and ASC 715, how should Pinnacle recognize Summit''s pension plan on the acquisition date?', '["Record the net pension liability of $1,800,000 as reported on Summit''s pre-acquisition balance sheet", "Record a net pension liability of $1,180,000 after eliminating the AOCI balance", "Record plan assets of $7,400,000 and a PBO of $7,400,000 with the $1,800,000 as goodwill", "Record a net pension liability of $1,800,000 at fair value with Summit''s AOCI balances eliminated and not carried forward"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 805-20-25-20 and ASC 715-30-35-62 require the acquirer to measure the pension obligation and plan assets at fair value on the acquisition date. The net funded status ($9.2M - $7.4M = $1.8M liability) is recognized on Pinnacle''s balance sheet. Summit''s AOCI items do not carry forward — they are subsumed into the acquisition-date fair value measurement. Choice A reaches the right amount but for the wrong reason. Choice B incorrectly nets AOCI against the liability.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Alternative amortization method faster than corridor
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Dalton Manufacturing has used the corridor approach for amortizing net actuarial losses since plan inception. The CFO proposes switching to immediate recognition of all gains and losses in pension expense. The cumulative unrecognized net loss is $1,500,000, the beginning PBO is $10,000,000, and plan assets are $8,000,000. Under ASC 715, what is the most accurate assessment of this proposal?', '["The change is permitted because ASC 715 allows any systematic method that amortizes gains and losses at least as fast as the corridor approach", "The change is prohibited because ASC 715 mandates the corridor approach as the only acceptable method", "The change is permitted only if the company simultaneously changes its approach for prior service cost amortization", "The change would require restatement of all prior periods under ASC 250 regardless of impracticability"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 715-30-35-25 states that any systematic method of amortizing gains and losses is acceptable, provided it results in amortization at least as rapid as the corridor approach, is applied consistently, and treats gains and losses symmetrically. Immediate recognition qualifies as such a method. Choice B is incorrect — the corridor is the minimum threshold, not the exclusive method. This is an accounting policy change under ASC 250, but the standard does not require restatement as an absolute (Choice D).', 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — OPEB with wholly unfunded plan (no expected return)
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Clearwater Industries provides postretirement healthcare benefits through a wholly unfunded OPEB plan. The beginning APBO is $3,400,000, the discount rate is 4.5%, service cost is $185,000, and amortization of prior service cost is $22,000. What is Clearwater''s net periodic postretirement benefit cost?', '["$185,000", "$338,000", "$360,000", "$207,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-60-35, net periodic postretirement benefit cost follows the same five-component SIRAE structure as pension expense. For a wholly unfunded plan, expected return on plan assets is zero. Calculation: service cost $185,000 + interest cost ($3,400,000 x 4.5% = $153,000) + PSC amortization $22,000 = $360,000. Choice B ($338,000) omits the PSC amortization. Choice A includes only service cost, ignoring interest and amortization components.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Measurement date requirement
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715, at what date must an employer measure the plan assets and benefit obligations of a defined benefit pension plan?', '["The date of the most recent actuarial valuation", "The employer''s fiscal year-end date", "The plan''s fiscal year-end date even if it differs from the employer''s", "Any date within three months of the employer''s fiscal year-end"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 715-30-35-63 requires that the measurement date for plan assets and benefit obligations be the employer''s fiscal year-end date (the date of the statement of financial position). Prior to the measurement date provisions effective in 2008, companies could use dates up to three months before year-end (Choice D). Choice C is incorrect because the plan''s fiscal year is irrelevant — the employer''s reporting date controls.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Multiemployer plan withdrawal liability
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Garrison Construction participates in a multiemployer defined benefit plan with three other employers. Garrison decides to withdraw and is assessed a withdrawal liability of $2,100,000 under ERISA based on its proportionate share of unfunded vested benefits. Garrison has been recognizing only annual contributions as pension expense. Under ASC 715-80 and ASC 450, how should Garrison account for the withdrawal?', '["Debit pension expense and credit cash for $2,100,000 when paid", "Recognize the liability only after a court confirms the assessment amount", "Record $2,100,000 as a prior period adjustment to retained earnings", "Recognize a loss and liability of $2,100,000 when withdrawal is probable and the amount is reasonably estimable"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 715-80-35-3 and ASC 450-20-25 require that a withdrawal liability from a multiemployer plan be recognized when the withdrawal is probable and the amount is reasonably estimable. The liability is recorded at the estimated assessment amount, not deferred until payment. Choice A incorrectly uses cash-basis accounting. Choice B applies an overly restrictive threshold — probable and estimable is sufficient without court confirmation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Contribution exceeds expense creating prepaid pension
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Brookfield Corp.''s net periodic pension cost for the year is $520,000. During the year, Brookfield contributes $600,000 to the plan trust. At the beginning of the year the prepaid pension cost balance was zero. What pension-related balance results from these transactions on the year-end balance sheet?', '["Prepaid pension cost (asset) of $80,000", "Accrued pension liability of $80,000", "Prepaid pension cost (asset) of $600,000", "No balance because the contribution offsets the expense entirely"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-30-25, when employer contributions exceed net periodic pension cost, the excess creates a prepaid pension cost asset. Brookfield contributed $600,000 against $520,000 of expense, resulting in a prepaid asset of $80,000. Note that the funded status model adjusts this further for OCI items, but from the expense-vs-contribution perspective the result is a prepaid asset. Choice B reverses the relationship, and Choice D incorrectly assumes contributions always equal expense.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Market-related value used for expected return computation
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Whitfield Corp. uses a five-year smoothed market-related value of plan assets to compute the expected return component of pension cost. At the beginning of the year, the fair value of plan assets is $5,200,000 and the market-related value is $4,800,000. The expected long-term rate of return is 7%. What expected return is subtracted in computing Whitfield''s net periodic pension cost?', '["$364,000", "$350,000", "$336,000", "$280,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-23, an employer that elects to use a market-related value (MRV) of plan assets computes the expected return using the MRV: $4,800,000 x 7% = $336,000. The MRV smooths asset gains and losses over up to five years, producing a return component that may differ from one based on current fair value. Choice A ($364,000) incorrectly applies the rate to fair value ($5,200,000 x 7%), which would be correct only if the employer elected to use fair value rather than MRV for this calculation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Salary progression rate explains PBO vs ABO difference
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Meridian Corp.''s actuary measures the pension obligation using a 5% discount rate and a 3% salary progression rate. The CFO asks why the PBO exceeds the ABO by $1,400,000. What explains this difference?', '["The PBO uses a lower discount rate than the ABO", "The PBO includes benefits for non-vested employees while the ABO excludes them", "The PBO includes future interest accruals while the ABO does not", "The PBO incorporates projected future salary increases while the ABO uses current salary levels"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 715-30-35, the projected benefit obligation differs from the accumulated benefit obligation solely because the PBO incorporates projected future salary increases into the benefit formula. Both measures use the same discount rate (Choice A is wrong) and both include non-vested employees — Choice B describes the VBO distinction instead. Choice C is incorrect because interest accruals are a component of PBO change over time, not the basis for the PBO-ABO divergence.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Cliff vesting definition
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under a defined benefit pension plan with cliff vesting, when do employees become entitled to their accrued pension benefits?', '["Only after completing the specified service period, at which point 100% of accrued benefits vest", "Incrementally over a graduated schedule based on years of service", "Immediately upon the date of hire with no service requirement", "Upon reaching the plan''s normal retirement age regardless of years of service"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ERISA and ASC 715-30, cliff vesting means employees have zero vested benefits until they complete the required service period (typically 3 or 5 years under ERISA), at which point 100% of accrued benefits become vested. Choice B describes graded (graduated) vesting, where vesting occurs incrementally (e.g., 20% per year). Choice C describes immediate vesting, which is not required by most plans.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Purpose of delayed recognition (smoothing)
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'What is the primary purpose of the delayed recognition mechanisms in ASC 715 for defined benefit pension accounting?', '["To allow employers to defer pension contributions until benefits are paid", "To reduce volatility in reported pension expense by spreading actuarial gains and losses over future periods", "To match pension expense with the employer''s cash contributions to the plan trust", "To ensure that pension expense equals the change in funded status each period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 715-30-35 employs delayed recognition for actuarial gains/losses and prior service cost to prevent excessive period-to-period volatility in pension expense. Items are initially recognized in OCI and amortized to expense over future periods. Choice A confuses accounting recognition with funding decisions. Choice C incorrectly equates expense with cash contributions — GAAP pension expense and funding are independent under ASC 715.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Postretirement life insurance within OPEB scope
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715-60, which of the following postretirement benefits falls within the scope of other postretirement employee benefits (OPEB) accounting?', '["Severance payments upon involuntary termination", "Deferred compensation arrangements payable after retirement", "Employer-provided group life insurance for retirees", "Stock-based compensation awards that vest at retirement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'ASC 715-60-15-2 defines OPEB as postretirement benefits other than pensions, including healthcare benefits, life insurance, and other welfare benefits provided to retirees. Severance payments (Choice A) fall under ASC 420 (exit/disposal costs). Deferred compensation (Choice B) is addressed by ASC 710. Stock-based compensation (Choice D) is governed by ASC 718, not ASC 715-60.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Current vs noncurrent classification of pension liability
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Kendrick Industries has an underfunded defined benefit plan with a PBO of $14,000,000 and plan assets of $11,200,000. The company estimates $380,000 in pension benefits will be paid to retirees within the next 12 months. How should Kendrick classify the $2,800,000 net pension liability on its balance sheet?', '["Current liability of $380,000 and noncurrent liability of $2,420,000", "Entirely as a current liability because retiree benefits are payable on demand", "Current liability of $2,800,000 because the plan is underfunded", "Noncurrent liability of $2,800,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 715-30-45-2, the net pension liability is classified as current only to the extent that benefits expected to be paid in the next 12 months exceed the fair value of plan assets available. Because plan assets ($11.2M) far exceed near-term benefits ($380K), no current portion exists and the entire $2,800,000 is classified as noncurrent. Choice A incorrectly splits the liability based on near-term payments regardless of plan asset coverage.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Discount rate selection: high-quality fixed-income
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715, what type of rate is used as the discount rate to calculate the present value of defined benefit pension obligations?', '["The rate available on high-quality fixed-income investments with similar payment timing", "The expected long-term rate of return on the plan''s invested assets", "The federal funds rate on the measurement date", "The employer''s weighted-average cost of capital"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 715-30-35-43 requires the discount rate to reflect rates at which pension benefits could be effectively settled — specifically the rate of return on high-quality fixed-income investments (typically AA-rated corporate bonds) with maturities matching expected benefit payments. Choice B describes the expected return on plan assets, a separate assumption. Choice D applies to corporate finance, not pension obligation measurement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Negative prior service cost from benefit reduction with existing PSC
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Ellsworth Corp. amends its defined benefit plan to reduce the benefit multiplier from 1.5% to 1.2% of final average salary, reducing the PBO by $840,000. The plan has existing unamortized prior service cost of $300,000 in AOCI from a prior benefit increase. The average remaining service period is 10 years. How should Ellsworth account for this amendment?', '["Recognize a $540,000 gain immediately in pension expense", "Credit AOCI for $840,000 as negative prior service cost, first offsetting the existing $300,000 debit, then amortize the net $540,000 credit over 10 years", "Reduce the PBO by $840,000 and amortize the entire credit separately from the existing $300,000", "Recognize an $840,000 curtailment gain because benefits are being reduced"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-10, a plan amendment reducing benefits creates negative prior service cost (credit to AOCI). The $840,000 credit first offsets the existing $300,000 unamortized PSC debit, leaving a net $540,000 AOCI credit to be amortized over the remaining service period. Choice A incorrectly recognizes an immediate gain. Choice D mischaracterizes the amendment — a formula change is not a curtailment, which requires reduction in expected future service years.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Service cost is the present value of current-period benefits
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Paxton Corp.''s actuary calculates the defined benefit service cost as $420,000 for the current year. The undiscounted value of benefits earned during the year is $780,000. Under ASC 715, what explains the difference between these two amounts?', '["Service cost is the present value of benefits earned during the period, discounted at the high-quality corporate bond rate", "Service cost equals cash benefits expected to be paid for current-year service", "Service cost is calculated using the expected return on plan assets as the discount factor", "Service cost is reduced by the employer''s annual contribution to the plan trust"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ASC 715-30-35-4 defines service cost as the actuarial present value of benefits attributed to employee service during the current period, discounted using the same high-quality corporate bond rate applied to the PBO. Because retirement benefits are paid decades in the future, the present value ($420,000) is substantially less than the undiscounted amount ($780,000). Choice C confuses the expected return on assets with the discount rate used for obligations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Employer contributions do not affect pension expense
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Rosewood Corp. contributes $750,000 to its defined benefit pension plan trust during the year. Net periodic pension cost calculated under the SIRAE framework is $620,000. Which statement best describes the relationship between contributions and pension expense?', '["Pension expense must be adjusted to equal the contribution amount", "The $130,000 excess contribution reduces the following year''s pension expense", "Contributions affect the funded status reported on the balance sheet but do not change net periodic pension cost", "Excess contributions are recognized as a gain in other comprehensive income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-30-35, net periodic pension cost is determined by the five SIRAE components — service cost, interest cost, return on assets, amortization of PSC, and excess gain/loss amortization. Employer contributions are not a component of expense. Contributions reduce the net pension liability (or increase prepaid pension) on the balance sheet but have no direct effect on pension expense. Choice A incorrectly equates contributions with expense, a common misconception.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — OPEB full eligibility date attribution period
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Westfield Corp. provides postretirement healthcare to employees who retire after age 55 with at least 10 years of service. An employee hired at age 40 expects to retire at age 62. Under ASC 715-60, over what period is the expected postretirement benefit obligation attributed to this employee''s service?', '["From age 40 to age 62 (hire date to expected retirement date)", "From age 40 to age 65 (hire date to Medicare eligibility)", "From age 45 to age 55 (the 10-year service period ending at eligibility)", "From age 40 to age 55 (hire date to full eligibility date)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 715-60-35-5 requires OPEB costs to be attributed from the employee''s hire date to the full eligibility date. Full eligibility requires meeting all plan conditions simultaneously — both age 55 and 10 years of service. This employee reaches 10 years at age 50 but does not satisfy the age-55 requirement until age 55, making that the full eligibility date. The attribution period runs from age 40 (hire) to age 55. Choice A incorrectly extends attribution to retirement, which is irrelevant once full eligibility is reached.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: hard / L3 — Settlement triggers proportionate AOCI recognition
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Grayson Corp.''s defined benefit plan has a beginning PBO of $20,000,000 and an unrecognized net actuarial loss of $1,500,000 in AOCI. During the year, Grayson irrevocably settles $8,000,000 of the PBO by purchasing annuity contracts from an insurer. The settlement is significant. Under ASC 715, what amount of the unrecognized net loss must Grayson recognize immediately?', '["$1,500,000", "$600,000", "$150,000", "None because the corridor approach continues to apply"]'::jsonb, 1, 'ASC 715-30-35-82 requires that when a significant settlement occurs, a proportionate share of unrecognized net gains or losses be recognized immediately. The proportion equals the percentage of PBO settled: $8,000,000 / $20,000,000 = 40%. Recognized loss = $1,500,000 x 40% = $600,000. Choice A incorrectly recognizes the entire amount, which would apply only to a complete plan termination. Choice D is incorrect because settlement triggers immediate recognition, overriding corridor deferral.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Actual loss on plan assets deferred in OCI
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Hamilton Corp.''s defined benefit plan has beginning plan assets of $8,000,000 with an expected return rate of 6.5%. During the year, a market downturn causes an actual loss on plan assets of $200,000. How does this event affect Hamilton''s pension accounting?', '["The $720,000 shortfall between expected and actual returns is deferred as an actuarial loss in OCI while pension expense uses the $520,000 expected return", "The $200,000 actual loss is recognized immediately in pension expense", "Plan assets are impaired by $200,000 and pension expense includes the actual loss", "The $520,000 expected return is reversed so no return component appears in pension expense"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-22, the expected return on plan assets ($8,000,000 x 6.5% = $520,000) is the amount used in computing net periodic pension cost, regardless of actual performance. The difference between expected return ($520,000) and actual return (-$200,000) = $720,000 is recognized as an actuarial loss in OCI, subject to future corridor amortization. Choice B incorrectly substitutes actual for expected returns in expense.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Tax effects of pension-related OCI items
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Caldwell Industries recognizes a $500,000 net actuarial loss in other comprehensive income for its defined benefit plan. The effective tax rate is 25%. During the year, $40,000 of previously deferred net actuarial loss is amortized from AOCI to pension expense. Under ASC 220 and ASC 740, what are the tax effects of these pension-related OCI transactions?', '["No tax effect because pension OCI items are permanently non-deductible", "A deferred tax asset of $125,000 on the new OCI loss with no adjustment for the reclassification", "A deferred tax asset of $125,000 on the new OCI loss reduced by a $10,000 deferred tax reclassification for the amortized amount", "A deferred tax benefit of $125,000 recognized directly in pension expense"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 740-20-45 and ASC 220-10-45, tax effects follow the item to which they relate. The $500,000 new actuarial loss in OCI generates a deferred tax asset of $125,000 (recognized in OCI). When $40,000 is reclassified from AOCI to pension expense, the related deferred tax of $10,000 ($40,000 x 25%) is also reclassified. Choice A is incorrect — pension OCI items create deductible temporary differences. Choice B ignores the reclassification tax effect.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — What causes actuarial gains and losses
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Under ASC 715, what causes actuarial gains and losses in a defined benefit pension plan?', '["Changes in the employer''s contribution levels to the plan trust", "Amendments to the plan''s benefit formula", "Market fluctuations that alter the plan''s funded status ratio", "Differences between actuarial assumptions and actual experience or changes in assumptions"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 715-30-35-22 defines actuarial gains and losses as arising from two sources: (1) changes in actuarial assumptions such as the discount rate, mortality, or turnover, and (2) differences between expected and actual experience such as asset returns. Choice B describes prior service cost, not actuarial gains/losses. Choice A relates to funding decisions which do not directly generate actuarial gains or losses under the standard.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — New hires increase aggregate service cost
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Belford Corp. hires 200 new employees into its defined benefit pension plan at the start of the year, increasing total participants by 30%. Prior to the new hires, annual service cost was $600,000. All other actuarial assumptions remain unchanged. What is the most likely effect on Belford''s net periodic pension cost?', '["Service cost increases because the additional participants earn benefits during the year, directly increasing pension expense", "Service cost remains unchanged because the new hires have not yet met the plan''s vesting requirements", "Net periodic pension cost decreases because the corridor threshold increases with a larger PBO", "Interest cost decreases because the younger new hires extend the plan''s average duration"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-4, service cost is the present value of benefits attributed to employee service during the current period for all plan participants, regardless of vesting status. Adding 200 participants means more employees earning benefits, increasing service cost and total pension expense. Choice B is incorrect — vesting affects the VBO but not the calculation of service cost. Benefits accrue and expense is recognized from the hire date.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L3 — Mortality assumption change produces actuarial loss in OCI
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Stratford Corp.''s defined benefit plan has a PBO of $12,000,000. The actuary updates mortality tables to reflect longer retiree life expectancies, increasing the PBO by $680,000. Under ASC 715, how is this adjustment recognized?', '["As an increase to pension expense of $680,000 in the current period", "As an actuarial loss of $680,000 in other comprehensive income subject to future corridor amortization", "As prior service cost of $680,000 in OCI amortized over remaining service life", "As a restatement of the beginning PBO with a cumulative catch-up adjustment to retained earnings"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-22, changes in actuarial assumptions — including mortality tables — produce actuarial gains or losses recognized in other comprehensive income. The $680,000 PBO increase from longer life expectancy is an actuarial loss recorded in OCI, subject to corridor amortization in future periods. Choice C is incorrect because prior service cost arises only from plan amendments, not assumption changes. Choice A incorrectly bypasses OCI deferral.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Defined contribution plan advantage for employer
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'What is the primary advantage to an employer of sponsoring a defined contribution plan rather than a defined benefit plan?', '["Defined contribution plans provide larger tax deductions for the employer", "Employees receive guaranteed retirement income under defined contribution plans", "The employer''s obligation is limited to specified contributions with no investment risk", "Defined contribution plans are exempt from ERISA regulatory requirements"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-70, a defined contribution plan specifies only the employer''s contribution amount or formula. The investment risk and retirement benefit adequacy fall entirely on the employee. The employer''s expense equals its required contribution, with no ongoing obligation for plan underfunding. Choice B is incorrect — defined benefit plans guarantee specific benefits while defined contribution plans do not. Choice D is wrong because DC plans are subject to ERISA.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Full plan termination: total loss including all deferred items
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Atlas Manufacturing terminates its defined benefit pension plan. On the termination date the plan has a PBO of $16,000,000, plan assets of $13,500,000, unrecognized net actuarial losses of $2,200,000, and unamortized prior service cost of $450,000 in AOCI. Atlas purchases group annuity contracts costing $16,400,000 to irrevocably settle all obligations. What total loss must Atlas recognize upon plan termination?', '["$2,500,000", "$2,650,000", "$2,900,000", "$3,050,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-79, a complete plan termination requires immediate recognition of all deferred pension items. The total loss includes: (1) settlement premium of $400,000 ($16.4M annuity cost minus $16.0M PBO), (2) unrecognized net actuarial loss of $2,200,000, and (3) unamortized prior service cost of $450,000 — totaling $3,050,000. Choice A captures only the funded status shortfall already on the balance sheet. Choice B includes only the AOCI items without the settlement premium.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: easy / L2 — Reclassification from AOCI to pension expense: net zero on total comprehensive income
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Glendale Corp. amortizes $35,000 of prior service cost from accumulated other comprehensive income to pension expense during the year. How does this reclassification affect the financial statements?', '["Total comprehensive income decreases by $35,000", "Pension expense increases by $35,000 and AOCI decreases by $35,000 with no effect on total comprehensive income", "Both pension expense and other comprehensive income increase by $35,000", "Retained earnings decrease by $35,000 with an offsetting increase in AOCI"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 715-30-35-25 and ASC 220-10-45, amortization of prior service cost from AOCI is a reclassification adjustment. It increases pension expense (reducing net income by $35,000) and simultaneously removes $35,000 from OCI (a positive OCI adjustment). The net effect on total comprehensive income is zero — the loss was already recognized in OCI when originally recorded. Choice A is incorrect because the OCI credit offsets the income statement charge.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — OPEB sensitivity: discount rate and healthcare trend rate interaction
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Pacific Medical Group sponsors an OPEB plan providing postretirement healthcare. The actuary reports that a 50-basis-point decrease in the discount rate would increase the APBO by $620,000, while a 100-basis-point increase in the healthcare cost trend rate would increase the APBO by $840,000. Under ASC 715-60, which statement best describes these effects?', '["Both changes increase the APBO because a lower discount rate raises present values and a higher trend rate increases projected benefit payments, amplifying each other when combined", "Only the discount rate affects the APBO; the healthcare trend rate affects only current-period service cost", "The effects offset because a lower discount rate reduces projected benefit growth", "The healthcare cost trend rate has no effect on the APBO after the ultimate rate is reached"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 715-60-35, the APBO is sensitive to both the discount rate and the healthcare cost trend rate. A lower discount rate increases the present value of future benefit payments, while a higher trend rate increases the projected magnitude of those payments. When combined, these effects amplify the APBO increase. ASC 715-60-50 requires disclosure of trend rate sensitivity. Choice B is incorrect — the trend rate directly affects the APBO by projecting higher per-capita claims costs over the entire benefit period.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L2 — Funded status is what appears on balance sheet, not adjusted for AOCI
(2, 'Employee Benefit Plans', 'Crawford Corp.''s defined benefit plan has a PBO of $9,500,000 and plan assets of $8,100,000. The unamortized prior service cost is $320,000 and the cumulative unrecognized net actuarial loss is $780,000 in AOCI. Under ASC 715, what amount is reported on Crawford''s balance sheet as the net pension liability?', '["$300,000 after netting the AOCI items against the underfunding", "$2,500,000 including both funded status and AOCI amounts", "$1,400,000", "$1,100,000 representing the AOCI items only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 715-30-25-1, the balance sheet reports the funded status — fair value of plan assets minus PBO: $8,100,000 - $9,500,000 = $(1,400,000). This underfunded amount of $1,400,000 is the net pension liability. The unamortized prior service cost ($320,000) and net actuarial loss ($780,000) are reported separately in accumulated other comprehensive income, not added to or netted against the funded status. Choice A incorrectly reduces the liability by AOCI items.', 'medium', 2)
;

COMMIT;
